<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Benin (restored)</h1><p class="st-meta">1914 CE – present · capital Benin City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The monarchy of Benin, restored by the British in 1914 after the sack of 1897, whose Oba remains a central figure of Edo culture in Nigeria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the British expedition of 1897 sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> and ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovonramwen">Oba Ovonramwen</a> was exiled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-calabar">Calabar</a>. When he died in 1914, the British allowed his son to succeed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eweka-ii">Eweka II</a>, restoring the monarchy within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a> as the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> Native Authority.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eweka-ii">Eweka II</a> rebuilt the royal palace and revived court ceremonies and the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, including the bronze casters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igun-street">Igun Street</a>, who continue to make brass works. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> had a recognised role in local government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, though real authority lay with British officials. Oba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akenzua-ii">Akenzua II</a> (1933-1978) took part in Nigerian politics in the late colonial period.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After Nigerian independence the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> remained a traditional ruler, today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>, with ceremonial and religious authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erediauwa">Erediauwa</a> reigned from 1979 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-ii">Ewuare II</a> from 2016, amid campaigns that have brought the return of some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> looted in 1897.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1914 CE – present"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.79%;width:2.21%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1914 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Benin City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eweka II, Akenzua II, Ewuare II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Edo traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Edo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Benin Kingdom, Benin Native Authority</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1970&amp;focus=kingdom-of-benin-restored" aria-label="Kingdom of Benin (restored) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4836 1941 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Benin (restored) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5156 2197L5155 2197M5156 2197L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5155 2197h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5156 2197h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5156 2197h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Benin City. <a href="/atlas?year=1970&amp;focus=kingdom-of-benin-restored">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
